10:46:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Replacing all of someone’s blood usually would not cure HIV or most cancers because the disease is not only in the circulating blood. - **HIV:** HIV hides and replicates inside immune cells throughout the body, especially in lymph nodes, gut tissue, and “reservoir” cells. Even if nearly all circulating blood were replaced, infected cells in tissues would repopulate the new blood. Modern HIV medicines suppress the virus very effectively; a complete cure generally requires eliminating those hidden reservoirs. - **Cancer:** Most cancers are solid tumors located in organs or tissues. Replacing blood does not remove the tumor. For blood cancers such as leukemia or lymphoma, the abnormal cells often arise in the **bone marrow** and lymphatic system, which continually make new blood cells. In some cases, doctors use intensive chemotherapy/radiation followed by a **stem-cell or bone-marrow transplant** to replace the blood-forming system—but this is high-risk and only appropriate for certain diseases. - **It is dangerous and difficult:** A person has roughly 5 liters of blood, but “washing out” or exchanging it all can cause bleeding, clotting problems, immune reactions, infection, electrolyte shifts, and organ stress. Donor blood also has limited lifespan and does not replace the patient’s immune system, marrow, or diseased tissues. There *are* situations where doctors partly or fully exchange blood—for example, severe newborn jaundice, certain poisonings, sickle-cell complications, or some rare blood disorders. But it is used when removing harmful material from the bloodstream actually addresses the problem.
