10:46:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The oil-change analogy makes sense for illnesses where the main problem is something circulating in the blood. In fact, medicine sometimes does essentially that: **exchange transfusion**, **plasma exchange**, and **dialysis-like blood filtering** can remove toxins, abnormal antibodies, excess bilirubin, or damaged blood cells and replace fluids or blood components. But blood is not just a fluid that gets dirty. It is a living, constantly renewed system: - **Bone marrow makes new blood cells continuously.** If the marrow is diseased—as in leukemia—it can rapidly produce more abnormal cells after an exchange. - **Many causes of illness live in tissues, not just blood.** HIV persists in immune cells in lymph nodes, gut, and marrow. Most cancers are masses of abnormal cells in organs or tissues. Swapping blood does not remove those cells. - **The “new” blood is temporary.** Transfused red cells eventually wear out; donor immune cells generally do not replace the person’s immune system long-term. - **Replacement blood can also be infected or damaged again.** HIV remaining in body reservoirs can infect new immune cells. A tumor can keep releasing cancer-related substances or cancer cells into the circulation. - **A full exchange has real risks:** immune incompatibility reactions, clotting/bleeding problems, fluid and electrolyte shifts, infection, and strain on the heart and lungs. So fresh blood can make someone feel and function better when they are severely anemic, bleeding, or have a blood-component problem—much like topping up or replacing an important fluid. But to cure HIV or cancer, doctors need to eliminate or control the **source**: the virus’s infected-cell reservoirs, the tumor, or the abnormal marrow that keeps generating diseased cells.
