10:47:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is a hopeful idea—and sometimes it really is close to that simple. Donated blood saves lives every day, and exchange transfusions can be dramatic, rapid treatments in the right situation. The reason doctors do not use it as a universal cure is not that they overlook the simple option. It is because the body’s problem may be in the blood, or it may be in the organs, marrow, immune cells, a tumor, or an infection living elsewhere. Replacing blood helps the first kind of problem; it cannot reliably erase the others. A useful way to put it is: - **Blood replacement can support the body and buy time.** - **Treatment of the source is what produces a lasting cure.** There are also many treatments that are simpler and safer than a full blood exchange. For HIV, daily or long-acting antiviral treatment can suppress the virus so well that people can live long lives and do not sexually transmit it when the virus is undetectable. For cancers, treatments are chosen based on where the cancer is and what drives it—sometimes surgery alone is enough; sometimes radiation, targeted drugs, immune therapy, or marrow transplant is needed. So the hopeful part is genuine: “fresh blood” is a powerful medical resource. It just has to be matched to the kind of illness it can actually fix.
