10:47:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Blood has long been associated with life, vitality, and health in many cultures, so the intuition is understandable. Blood loss clearly makes people weak, and restoring blood after major bleeding can visibly bring someone back from the brink. But historical beliefs about “purifying” or renewing a life force were not reliable medical evidence. - **Bloodletting** was used for centuries under the idea that illness came from an imbalance of bodily fluids. It occasionally appeared to help by coincidence or in a few unusual conditions, but for most sick people it did not purify anything and often made them worse by causing anemia, shock, or infection. - **Transfusions** became useful only after medicine learned about blood groups, compatibility testing, sterile technique, and blood storage. Before that, transfusions were often dangerous or fatal. - Modern transfusion medicine kept the useful part of the intuition—replacing blood when blood or a blood component is truly the problem—while testing it carefully to determine when it helps and when it harms. So “healthy blood can restore someone” is not nonsense; it is literally true in situations such as hemorrhage, severe anemia, some clotting disorders, and certain exchange-transfusion conditions. The part medicine does not support is the broader claim that blood is a universal carrier of sickness that can simply be drained and replaced to cure diseases whose source remains in the marrow, organs, immune system, tumor, or infected tissues.
