10:47:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Blood does reach nearly every organ, but circulation is not the same as flushing out and replacing the cells of those organs. A better analogy is a river flowing through a city: replacing the river water may remove something dissolved in the water, but it does not remove mold inside buildings, a factory releasing pollution, or damaged machinery along the riverbank. Those sources can contaminate the new water again. In the body: - **Blood is inside blood vessels.** Most cells of the body—including tumor cells in a tumor, infected immune cells in lymph nodes, and cells in bone marrow—are outside the circulating blood. - **HIV is inside cells.** Even if virus particles and infected cells in the bloodstream were greatly reduced, infected cells in lymph nodes, gut, marrow, and other tissue can start producing virus again and infect the new blood cells. - **Cancer is made of abnormal body cells growing in tissue.** Replacing blood does not remove the tumor. If it is leukemia, the abnormal cells may be continually produced by diseased bone marrow. - **Diseased organs can keep releasing the problem.** An infected tissue, inflamed organ, toxin-producing bacteria, or damaged marrow can reintroduce harmful material into fresh blood. Also, a literal total replacement is not quite achievable in the simple “drain and refill” sense. Blood is continuously circulating, tissues exchange fluid and cells with it, and the body is constantly making new blood cells. Exchange transfusion can replace a large fraction of circulating blood, but it cannot replace every cell or sterilize every tissue. Where the harmful thing is mainly *in the blood itself*, exchange can work very well—for example, removing excess bilirubin in some newborns, reducing sickled red cells in certain sickle-cell emergencies, or removing particular antibodies or toxins. But for a lasting cure, doctors have to stop or remove the source that would otherwise contaminate or repopulate the replacement blood.
