10:48:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s reasonable to value transfusion as a powerful, practical treatment. But the claim that replacing blood cures sickness *in general* is not supported by medical evidence and could be dangerous if it leads someone to delay effective care. A full blood exchange is a real but invasive procedure, with risks such as severe transfusion reactions, fluid/electrolyte disturbances, bleeding or clotting complications, infection, and heart strain. Doctors use it when there is evidence that the harmful substance or cells are primarily in the blood and that removing them will help. For many illnesses, “fresh blood” may temporarily improve weakness or anemia, but it cannot remove the cause: - Pneumonia persists in the lungs. - Appendicitis persists in the appendix. - Diabetes persists because of insulin production/action problems. - HIV persists in infected cells throughout tissues. - Most cancer persists in tumors. - Leukemia can persist in marrow that keeps making abnormal blood cells. The practical approach is not rejecting blood replacement; it is using it where it fits, alongside treating the actual source of disease.
