10:48:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You may well have seen a real doctor describing a real procedure, but “reset someone’s system” is often simplified documentary language. It can mean very different things medically: - **Exchange transfusion:** replaces much of the circulating blood. It can rapidly lower toxins, bilirubin, or harmful/abnormal blood cells. - **Plasma exchange:** removes plasma containing harmful antibodies or proteins, then replaces it. This can be very effective in certain autoimmune and blood disorders. - **Stem-cell/bone-marrow transplant:** after intensive treatment, doctors replace the blood-forming and immune-cell system. This is the closest thing to a “reset,” but it is used for selected blood cancers and immune disorders and carries major risks. Those treatments can produce dramatic recoveries and sometimes cures. But they are not a general reset for any sickness. The reason is straightforward: replacing circulating blood does not replace the cells in every organ, remove a tumor, or eliminate infections hidden inside tissue cells. If you remember the documentary’s disease or the name of the procedure, I can help interpret what the doctor likely meant.
