10:50:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: HIV involves the immune system, but it is not the same kind of problem as a blood cancer or an autoimmune disorder. A marrow/stem-cell transplant can sometimes cure certain leukemias because the diseased blood-forming cells are destroyed and replaced, and the donor immune system may eliminate remaining leukemia cells. But HIV is a virus that infects immune cells throughout the body and can remain dormant inside long-lived cells in lymph nodes, gut tissue, brain-related reservoirs, and marrow. There have been a very small number of documented HIV cures after stem-cell transplants performed for life-threatening blood cancers. In those cases, the transplant process was extremely intensive, and some donors had a rare **CCR5-delta-32** genetic variant that makes many immune cells resistant to the most common strains of HIV. This is not comparable to a routine transfusion or a general “immune-system reset,” and the transplant itself carries substantial risks, including potentially fatal complications. For almost everyone with HIV, antiretroviral therapy is far safer and highly effective. It can reduce HIV to an undetectable level, allowing a long life and preventing sexual transmission when viral suppression is maintained (“undetectable = untransmittable,” or U=U). So the general principle is real—replacing the right blood/immune system can sometimes cure selected diseases—but whether it works depends on the specific cause. HIV requires eliminating infected reservoirs and preventing infection of replacement immune cells; fresh blood alone does neither.
