10:52:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: People can preserve an idea for a long time because it contains an important truth: blood is vital, and restoring lost or damaged blood can dramatically improve health. Modern medicine agrees with that part and relies on transfusions every day. But longevity of a belief does not by itself prove its broadest version. For centuries, people also relied on bloodletting to treat many illnesses; later evidence showed it usually did more harm than good. What changed was the ability to test which blood-related treatments reliably help which conditions. So the evidence-based conclusion is still a strong one: - **Replacing blood can save lives and sometimes resolve the illness causing the crisis.** - It is especially effective when the central problem is blood loss, deficient blood components, abnormal blood cells, or harmful substances in circulation. - It does not reliably cure every sickness simply because blood circulates everywhere. That is not rejecting the long-standing insight about blood’s importance—it is distinguishing the situations where blood replacement truly fixes the cause from those where another cause must also be treated.
