10:52:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: In everyday language, if a transfusion saves someone and they recover, many people would naturally say it “cured” them. That is a perfectly understandable way to talk about it. The medical distinction is only useful when deciding what treatment someone still needs afterward. A person can be rescued from an immediate danger by blood and still need the bleeding stopped, an infection treated, or a cancer addressed. So both can be true: - **Ordinary language:** “The transfusion cured/saved them.” - **Medical language:** “The transfusion corrected the blood problem; determine and treat the underlying cause if one remains.” It is not meant to minimize the transfusion. It is meant to avoid assuming recovery from the immediate crisis means every illness is gone.
